---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import { Card, Badge, Alert } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Intuition Cheatsheet - Replenish Guide | Premodern MTG"
    image="/src/assets/banners/intuition.jpeg"
    description="Master the art of Intuition in Replenish. Discover common pile patterns, engine setups, and complex situational plays to maximize your card selection."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="The Intuition Cheatsheet"
        description="<strong>A few tips:</strong> Intuition takes real skill, and it'll take a while to learn every line it opens up, since it does so many jobs over the course of a game. The core tradeoff is now versus later: when you tutor for a non-combo piece (like removal or a sideboard card), you're pulling three copies out of your deck to guarantee one in hand right now. Keep this in mind when building your deck, too—anything you want to reliably fetch with Intuition needs at least 3 copies in your 75."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/intuition.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Intuition"
        imageCaption="Intuition by April Lee"
        isCard={true}
    />
    <Card title="Core Mechanics: Hand vs. Graveyard Enforcement" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            Your opponent picks which card goes to your hand and which two hit
            the graveyard, so you have to build your pile with the right
            redundancy for where you want each card to end up:
        </p>

        <div
            style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.5rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;"
        >
            <div>
                <h3
                    style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-10); font-size: 1.2rem;"
                >
                    Enforce to Hand
                </h3>
                <p style="margin: 0;">
                    When you need a specific answer or threat in hand right away
                    (spot removal, a sideboard card), your pile has to be <strong
                        >3 identical copies</strong
                    > or 3 cards that do the same job. That's why any silver bullet
                    you want to reliably grab with <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a
                    > needs at least 3 copies in your 75.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div>
                <h3
                    style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-10); font-size: 1.2rem;"
                >
                    Enforce to Graveyard
                </h3>
                <p style="margin: 0;">
                    When you just need a combo enchantment in the graveyard for
                    an upcoming <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                    >, the pile only needs <strong>2 copies</strong> of that card.
                    Whichever one the opponent puts in your hand, at least one is
                    guaranteed to hit the yard.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div style="margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            <Alert theme="warning">
                <strong>Incomplete Piles & Deck Tracking</strong><br />
                If you've already drawn a copy of a card you really need in
                hand (leaving only two in your library), pair those two with a
                high-value threat like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                > to put your opponent in a lose-lose spot. Always keep track of what's
                left in your deck before you resolve Intuition, so you never build a
                pile you can't use.
            </Alert>
        </div>
    </Card>

    <Card title="General Heuristics & Timing" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            <strong>Post-Sideboard Role Shift & On-Stack Hate:</strong> Against
            permanent graveyard hate like <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Phyrexian Furnace"
                >Phyrexian Furnace</a
            >, or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Planar Void"
                >Planar Void</a
            >, don't build combo-dumping piles blind. Intuition stops being a
            graveyard-stocking tool and becomes a removal tutor instead—grab
            three copies of <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
            > or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Disenchant"
                >Disenchant</a
            > to clear out their graveyard hate before you go back to assembling the
            combo.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-0">
            <strong>Deck Depletion & Managing Threat Density:</strong> In long
            attrition games against control, be careful not to over-tutor.
            Intuition pulls cards three at a time, and with <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
            > and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Frantic Search"
                >Frantic Search</a
            > looting on top of that, you can burn through your win conditions or
            even risk decking out if your first few reanimation attempts get
            countered. Know when to stop thinning your deck and start conserving
            what's left.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Common Tactical Piles" titleTag="h2">
        <p style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            Here are the standard piles and when to reach for each one:
        </p>

        <div
            style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;"
        >
            <div
                style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(150, 150, 150, 0.2); padding-bottom: 1.5rem;"
            >
                <div
                    style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap;"
                >
                    <h3 style="margin: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);">
                        Dumping Combo Pieces
                    </h3>
                    <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}
                        >Enforce to Graveyard</Badge
                    >
                </div>
                <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                    Your bread-and-butter setup when you're holding <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                    > plus a discard outlet (<a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                    > or <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                    >). If a particular enchantment has to hit the graveyard now
                    to set up your untap, put at least two copies in the pile.
                </p>
                <div
                    class="callout"
                >
                    <strong>Standard Configurations:</strong>
                    <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence"
                        >Opal</a
                    > + <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree</a
                    > + <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Wave</a
                    >
                    (balanced lock), <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Opalescence">Opal</a
                    > + <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Opalescence">Opal</a
                    > + <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Wave</a
                    > (guaranteed Opal in yard/hand), or <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Wave</a
                    > x3 (when fighting explosive aggression).
                </div>
            </div>

            <div
                style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(150, 150, 150, 0.2); padding-bottom: 1.5rem;"
            >
                <div
                    style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap;"
                >
                    <h3 style="margin: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);">
                        Split Piles: The Lose-Lose Dilemma
                    </h3>
                    <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}>Enforce to Hand</Badge
                    >
                </div>
                <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                    Later in the game you can drop the redundancy and build a
                    pile of three different cards that work together. If you pick
                    a combo where the enchantments you discard feed a
                    reanimation spell or a draw engine in the same pile, every
                    card the opponent could hand you still moves you
                    forward—a real lose-lose choice for them.
                </p>
                <div
                    class="callout"
                >
                    <strong>Standard Configurations:</strong>
                    <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                        >Replenish</a
                    > + <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                    > + <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Wave</a
                    > (if they give you Replenish, you immediately animate Attunement
                    and Wave; if they give you Attunement or Wave, you secure powerful
                    on-board engines).
                </div>
            </div>

            <div
                style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(150, 150, 150, 0.2); padding-bottom: 1.5rem;"
            >
                <div
                    style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap;"
                >
                    <h3 style="margin: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);">
                        Tutoring Targeted Removal
                    </h3>
                    <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}>Enforce to Hand</Badge
                    >
                </div>
                <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                    <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                        >Swords to Plowshares</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                    > answer completely different problems, so grab whichever one the
                    board calls for. Tutor for three Swords when a fast creature clock
                    is about to kill you. Tutor for three Seals to break through a lock
                    piece—and as a bonus, any Seals you discard come back for free off
                    your next <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                    >.
                </p>
                <div
                    class="callout"
                >
                    <strong>Standard Configurations:</strong>
                    <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                        >Swords to Plowshares</a
                    > x3 (anti-creature), <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                    > x3 (anti-artifact/enchantment).
                </div>
            </div>

            <div
                style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(150, 150, 150, 0.2); padding-bottom: 1.5rem;"
            >
                <div
                    style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap;"
                >
                    <h3 style="margin: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);">
                        Guaranteeing Land Drops & Deck Thinning
                    </h3>
                    <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}>Enforce to Hand</Badge
                    >
                </div>
                <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                    Early on, locking in a land drop keeps you from stumbling on
                    mana with an otherwise great hand. And in long games against
                    slow control, grabbing three lands thins your deck—pulling
                    dead lands out so your future draws are more likely to be
                    live threats and counters.
                </p>
                <div
                    class="callout"
                >
                    <strong>Standard Configurations:</strong>
                    <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Ancient Tomb"
                        >Ancient Tomb</a
                    > x3, <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Skycloud Expanse">Skycloud Expanse</a
                    > x3, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Plains"
                        >Plains</a
                    > x3.
                </div>
            </div>

            <div
                style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(150, 150, 150, 0.2); padding-bottom: 1.5rem;"
            >
                <div
                    style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap;"
                >
                    <h3 style="margin: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);">
                        Finding a Discard Engine
                    </h3>
                    <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}>Enforce to Hand</Badge
                    >
                </div>
                <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                    When your hand is clogged with combo enchantments and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                    > but has no way to discard them, use Intuition to grab a draw
                    engine and get your lines moving.
                </p>
                <div
                    class="callout"
                >
                    <strong>Standard Configurations:</strong>
                    <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                    > x3, <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                    > x3.
                </div>
            </div>

            <div>
                <div
                    style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap;"
                >
                    <h3 style="margin: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);">
                        Tutoring Sideboard Tech
                    </h3>
                    <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}>Enforce to Hand</Badge
                    >
                </div>
                <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                    In games 2 and 3, grabbing three copies of the right
                    sideboard card lets you shut off their counters, disable
                    their utility lands, or stop their combo cold whenever you
                    need to.
                </p>
                <div
                    class="callout"
                >
                    <strong>Standard Configurations:</strong>
                    <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                    > x3, <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                    > x3, <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
                    > x3.
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </Card>
</Layout>
